{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>These sinners against their own souls.</b> Perhaps it intends to remove from their hearts [any thought] lest they grieve over the matter being on their account. Also, lest any person of Israel think that they were the cause; therefore it says <b>these sinners against their own souls</b> — meaning, that they wounded themselves and destroyed their own souls, and there was no cause for it from anyone else. And even so, we find that Israel did not sense HaShem's intention in these matters, and they complained against Moses, etc. — אַתֶּם הֲמִתֶּם אֶת עַם ה׳ (Numbers 17:6)." "<b>For they brought them before YHWH and they became sanctified.</b> Meaning, the reason I command you to elevate the firepans is not that I was appeased by the incense they offered before Me, but rather by their bringing them — meaning, by the firepans themselves, and before they placed incense upon them — which is alien and abominable to Me — the firepans had already become sanctified. And that is what the verse says: <b>וַיִּקְדָּשׁוּ</b>. It is like one who brings silver or golden vessels to the Sanctuary — they become consecrated. And consider for yourself: if those who brought them had reconsidered and changed their minds and had not placed the incense on the fire, would the firepans have reverted to ordinary use? From this it follows that it was not because they placed incense upon them that the firepans lost their sanctity. And as for what Ramban argues against Rashi, and this is his language: 'This is like a layman who made service vessels to offer incense outside [the Sanctuary]' — concerning this the verse states ״הִקְרִיבוּם לִפְנֵי ה׳״ in the Tent of Meeting — meaning, that they consecrated them and brought them to the consecrated place."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:38.531381598Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}